Embedded React: scroll a ScrollView by moving its painted pixels.

Modified from Embedded React (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Cory Lamming) at the
revision pinned in dependencies.lock.json, after the patches listed before this
one there. Changed files: engine/include/native_renderer.h,
engine/core/native_renderer.c, engine/core/renderer_internal.h,
engine/scene/er_node_internal.h, engine/scene/compositor.c,
backends/software/renderer_backend.c and engine/tests/scroll (new
test_scroll_copy.c). Proposed upstream as pull request #293.

A changed scroll offset dirtied the ScrollView, so every commit of a scroll
repainted (and a host re-uploaded) the whole viewport. Backends may now offer
move_rect (the software backend does). When the viewport's pixels are moved
exactly by a translation, the commit moves them and repaints only the exposed
strip: the ancestors, the ScrollView included, must paint a solid,
translation-invariant underlay there (no gradient, shadow, opacity, transform,
or border band or corner inside the viewport); descendants' footprints move with
their pixels, and any other node painted into the viewport, and any vacated
footprint, is repainted both where it is and where the move carried it.
Anything else, a step of a viewport or more, multi-buffer and banded
backends, and backends without move_rect repaint the viewport as before. The
moved region is reported through the dirty rects. test_scroll_copy compares
single steps, a chain of copies, a scroll with overlapping changes and the
fallbacks against full repaints, pixel for pixel. This is the change proposed
upstream (branch commit 4e74624), which also keeps the painted offset of
ScrollViews beyond the eighth scrolling in one commit and resets the pending
queue in er_reset().

